            Healthy adults over 65 need about 1.0 to 1.2 g of protein per kilogram of body weight a day, according to the 2013 <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23867520/">PROT-AGE Study Group position paper</a> in the Journal of the American Medical Directors Association. That is 25 to 50% more than the 0.8 g/kg US Recommended Dietary Allowance, which was set for adults in general and is widely considered too low to protect muscle in later life. The same paper suggests 1.2 to 1.5 g/kg for older people with an acute or chronic illness, and up to 2.0 g/kg for those with severe illness, injury or marked malnutrition.
          </p>

          <p>
            European clinical nutrition experts reached the same range. The <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24814383/">ESPEN Expert Group recommendations</a> (Clinical Nutrition, 2014) call for at least 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg a day for healthy older people and 1.2 to 1.5 g/kg for those who are malnourished or at risk because of illness, alongside daily physical activity and resistance exercise where possible. The later <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30005900/">ESPEN guideline on clinical nutrition and hydration in geriatrics</a> (2019) uses about 1 g/kg as a guiding value to be adjusted for each person's health.
          </p>

          <p>
            The PROT-AGE group also made a per-meal point that explains why 30 g shakes exist: older muscle seems to need a bigger dose at one sitting to respond, and the paper suggests 25 to 30 g of protein per meal. Many seniors eat a light breakfast and lunch and most of their protein at dinner, so a single 30 g shake at breakfast fixes the weakest meal of the day rather than piling more onto the strongest.
          </p>

