          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What is the best protein drink for seniors?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most seniors, Ensure Max Protein is the best protein drink: 30 g of protein, 150 calories and 1 g of sugar per 11 fl oz shake, per Abbott's label. Premier Protein 30 g is a cheaper near-twin at about $1.77 per 25 g. Seniors who are losing weight usually need a calorie-dense drink such as Ensure Plus instead, ideally on a dietitian's advice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein should a 70-year-old drink in a shake?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  About 25 to 30 g per shake is the amount the PROT-AGE Study Group suggests per meal for older adults, which is why many senior-focused drinks carry 30 g. Total daily protein matters more: roughly 1.0 to 1.2 g per kilogram of body weight for healthy adults over 65, or about 70 to 84 g for a 154 lb person, unless a doctor sets a different target.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is Ensure or Boost better for the elderly?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Neither brand is better across the board; the right product line matters more than the brand. Ensure Max Protein and Premier suit stable weight, while Ensure Plus and BOOST Very High Calorie suit unwanted weight loss. BOOST High Protein, at 20 g of protein and 250 calories, sits between. Compare the calorie and protein lines, then pick the flavor the person will actually drink.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Can seniors drink a protein shake every day?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Most healthy seniors can drink one protein shake a day, and many benefit because their diets fall short of the 1.0 to 1.2 g/kg protein target. Daily use needs a doctor's input for anyone with kidney disease, diabetes on insulin, a fluid restriction or warfarin treatment. A shake should top up meals, not replace them, unless a dietitian advises it.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Are protein drinks good for elderly people with diabetes?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Low-sugar protein drinks can fit a diabetes plan. Glucerna Protein Smart lists 3 g of sugar and 7 g of carbohydrate, and Ensure Max Protein lists 1 g of sugar and 6 g of carbohydrate, per their labels. Calorie-dense drinks such as Ensure Plus carry about 47 g of carbohydrate, so they need to be counted carefully with a diabetes educator's help.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>What protein drink is best for a senior with no appetite?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  A small, calorie-dense bottle usually works best for a senior with very little appetite. BOOST Very High Calorie gives 530 calories and 22 g of protein in 8 fl oz, and Ensure Plus gives 350 calories and 16 g of protein, per their labels. Sipping through the day helps. Persistent loss of appetite or weight should always be checked by a doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Do protein shakes cause constipation in older adults?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Protein shakes do not usually cause constipation on their own, but replacing fiber-rich food with low-fiber drinks, plus low fluid intake, can. Ensure Max Protein and Glucerna Protein Smart list 4 g of fiber per shake, and BOOST High Protein with Fiber lists 3 g. Drinking water through the day helps too. Ongoing bowel changes deserve a conversation with a doctor.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
